Z||000002||ROB MAC||09-29-2000||04:38 PM||||Check around the head gasket. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Seanh||09-29-2000||06:32 PM||||I just tried it, and I still cant see where the damn stuff is commin from. I can see where its not comming from. I think its comming from somewhere above the A/C compressor. But the rubber hose going into the engine is not broken. Possibly the metal pipe it hooks to? Head gasket is not somthing I want to hear right now. LoL. My A/C system is not charged or filled, should I take the comprssor off and see if I can see anything? How hard it taking the compressor off? If it is indeed the water pump, do I have to take the engine out to replace it? It looks that way from what I can see. The leak just doesnt look in the right place to be comming from the weep hole, but I cant see much. Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000004||tgowens||09-30-2000||03:56 AM||||Seanh, wipe down the underside of the engine (oil pan, trans pan, etc.) and see if you can determine where the leak is coming from. There are may places coolant can leak from but I would suspect the water pump if it hasn't been replaced. -- and no, it is not necessary to remove the engine to remove the water pump. Disconnect the battery and the wiring harness connectors and move them out of the way. Then remove the drive pully from the water pump. there are three different sizes of torx head bolts in the water pump to remove (and there are several of them). If you install a new water pump be sure and seal the plug well (we use RTV here at the Fiero Shop) and use a thin bead on each side of the gasket when installing the new pump. Be sure also, that the new pump has a metal impeller rather than the plastic ones commonly found on may water pumps. -- but.......it <I>can</I> be removed without taking the engine out.<P>Oh yes, I would recommend that you wear a long sleeved shirt - that fiberglass insulation in the engine compartment will make you itch for days if you get it in your arms!!<P>------------------<BR>          <IMG SRC="http://www.tgowens.com/tgowenssig.gif">                      <BR><A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero" TARGET=_blank>www.tgowens.com/fiero</A> Blue '87 GT - Gold '87 GT||||reg||1
Z||000005||Seanh||09-30-2000||09:21 AM||||Thanks, I am going to remove the A/C Compressor today and see if I can see anything from there. I dont use it anyway, so Its just wasting a belt and space. I will also take the batt. out and look from above. Man I hate the insulation in the engine bay.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000006||Rodney||09-30-2000||10:52 AM||||Is it the steel coolant tube rusted thru? This is the black tube that runs from the upper passenger area to the lower drivers cooling tube. They are mild steel and rust. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Seanh||09-30-2000||12:38 PM||||I think that might be it. I still cant see until I remove the batt. today. I think you hit the nail on the head. It seems if it were leaking it would be right above the A/C compressor and that is the area it is leaking in, also, I see small spots of rust in other areas on it, but not leaking yet. Any place I can get a new pipe if this is the case? Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000008||Rodney||09-30-2000||02:42 PM||||If it is a 85-87 you may be able to get a new one from GM. But it will be painted steel. 88's are all gone. I have them for all years in 304 stainless steel. On my web page.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com" TARGET=_blank>www.rodneydickman.com</A> <P>Rodney||||reg||1
Z||000009||Seanh||10-01-2000||01:19 PM||||How do I remove my old one to check if it is indeed rusted and leaking?<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000010||Carrolles||10-01-2000||02:43 PM||||I had a similar leak on my 85SE v-6.  It seemed to be coming from the hose that connects the right side return pipe to the water pump inlet.  I removed the hose but it was ok.  With the engine up on jack stands and running I still could not see exactly where the leak was coming from even with mirrors.<P>I borrowed a radiator pressure tester kit from a friend.  I let the system cool down and made sure it was as full as possible with the tail end up in the air.  I hooked the pump up to the radiator cap and pumped it up to about 13 psi then got back under the car to look for the leak.  BTW I had removed the rr wheel, fender liner, and splash curtain to get better access.<P>I found coolant spewing from between the timing chain cover and the block.  It was where the coolant inlet is.  The gasket is very narrow there and had squeezed out.<P>Using a puller I got for $15.00 from Harbour Frieght and an electric impact wrench I got for $69.00 from them also I was able to get the crank pulley/harmonic damper off and pull the water pump and timing chain cover off.  It wasn't too difficult but it does take time.<P>So far so good.<P>I've seen the radiator pressure testers in JC Whitney for about $80.00. I was luck to have a friend with a shop who had one I could borrow.<P>Removing the A/C compressor would not give better access for pulling the water pump on a v-6.  Romoving the battery and its heat shield, the torque strut bracket that bolts to the front of the rear head, the lower A/C bracket, and the egr solenoid will make life easier.<P>Remember to mark which bolt goes where when you remove the water pump.  There are several different types and sizes that you don't want to mix up.  I traced the new water pump gasket onto a piece of cardbard and poked where all the bolts go.  As I removed a bolt I stuck it into the cardboard.<P>I got the timing cover gasket set from AutoZone for about $8.00.  It included the oil pan gasket front piece, timing chain cover gasket, water pump gasket, and timing chain cover crankshaft seal.<P>Hope this helps,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000011||Seanh||10-04-2000||06:05 PM||||Iv been pretty busy this week so I hevent been working on my car. I went to Advanced Auto Parts and got a gasket for the waterpump today. I am going to remove the water pump and check it out. Looking at it, it looks like it is leaking around the water pump, but not the weep hole. If it is the weep hole, how can I rebuild my water pump? I might just rebuild it anyway to save any trouble I might have down the line. I am hoping it is just the water pump gasket/seal, thats what it looks like at this point. (Fingers crossed) I will update once I get it checked out.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000012||Carrolles||10-05-2000||09:34 AM||||Rebuilt water pumps are cheap.  Autozone has them for around $25.00.<P>I got a new unit for $32.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Seanh||10-05-2000||07:16 PM||||Okay, I took the batt. out and all that other stuff your supposed to take out to remove the pump. I took off the big hose going into the top of the engine on the theromostat houseing. Anyway, I look under it and there is another small hose going in under the thermostat. I just cant seem to get it off, but thats not my bigger problem. I noticed under it, it looks like coolent has dripped there, and also you know where the top of the water pump bolts on, there looks like there has been coolent back there too. It looks to far to the left (looking at the engine from the back of the car) to be comming from the seal around the pump or the weep hole. But also tword the back of the car and water pump, there is another hose going to it, and there is coolent all over it too. I thought when I first looked at the coolent under the thermo housing, that it might be a gasket/seal around the metal pipe the 2 hoses hook to and it holds the thermostat and it hooks to the top of the engine (I think) Sorry for having to describe everthing like this but I am not good at things like this. Thanks for the help so far.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000014||Seanh||10-06-2000||04:38 PM||||Does anyone know of a way to test the cooling system since I dont have a pressure builder to hook on it, I cant start the car, I could put it back together and take it in town, but would risk overheating it? Any way to put pressure in the system with somthing I might have, like an Air Compressor maybe?<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
